{
  "version": 5,
  "name": "How-Ohms-Law-Works-Two-Resistors",
  "description": "Two separate loops share one 5 volt supply, so each resistor sees the full 5 volts. Ohm's law says current equals voltage divided by resistance. The 1 kilohm resistor carries 5 volts divided by 1,000 ohms, which is 5 milliamps (thousandths of an amp). The 2.2 kilohm resistor carries 5 volts divided by 2,200 ohms, which is about 2.27 milliamps. More resistance means less current at the same voltage. Place the meter on each resistor's lead to read its current, then compare the reading with your own division.",
  "wheelLocked": false,
